course: books
lesson: 51
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VII, part 3: The Piper at the Gates of Dawn (the gift of forgetfulness)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The sun's golden disc rises over the horizon and dazzles the two friends; when they can see again, the Vision has gone, and the air is full of birds singing in the dawn.
explain: A little breeze blows in their faces and brings the Piper's last gift, forgetfulness, so that the great memory will not overshadow their happy little lives; the Rat only says this seems «the right sort of place» and runs to the sleeping Portly.
explain: Portly wakes with a squeak of joy, then searches the island for the one who watched over him and cries when he cannot find him; the Rat stares at deep hoof-marks: «Some — great — animal — has been here».
explain: They row Portly back, set him down on the tow-path near the ford, and watch him waddle off until Otter leaps up from the shallows with an amazed and joyous bark.
explain: Drifting home, both feel strangely tired; the Rat hears the wind in the reeds turn into words, a song about helping and then forgetting, and falls asleep smiling before he can pass on the last, «real» part.
fact: «Sudden and magnificent, the sun's broad golden disc» rises facing them and dazzles them; when they can look again, «the Vision had vanished».
fact: Now «the air was full of the carol of birds that hailed the dawn»: the birds sing again (hailed = greeted).
fact: A breeze blows «lightly and caressingly in their faces; and with its soft touch came instant oblivion» (oblivion = complete forgetting).
fact: The book calls this «the last best gift that the kindly demi-god is careful to bestow»: «the gift of forgetfulness» (a demi-god is a half-god; bestow = give).
fact: Why? «Lest the awful remembrance should remain and grow»: so the huge memory would not «spoil all the after-lives of little animals», and they could be «happy and lighthearted as before» (lest = so that ... not; awful here = full of awe).
fact: The Mole, like someone waking from a beautiful dream, keeps only «a dim sense of the beauty of it, the beauty!», and then even that fades.
fact: Portly wakes «with a joyous squeak», then hunts round the island «with pleading whine», like a child who fell asleep in its nurse's arms and wakes alone, and at last cries bitterly.
fact: The Rat looks long at «certain hoof-marks deep in the sward» (sward = short grass) and murmurs: «Some — great — animal — has been here».
fact: Portly is comforted by the promise of «a jaunt on the river in Mr. Rat's real boat» (a jaunt = a short trip for fun).
fact: On the way back the flowers seem to have «less of richness and blaze of colour than they seemed to remember seeing quite recently somewhere — they wondered where».
fact: Near the ford they set Portly on the tow-path (the path along a river bank); Otter starts up from «his lonely vigil» (a vigil = staying awake to watch) with «his amazed and joyous bark».
fact: The Mole: «I feel as if I had been through something very exciting and rather terrible ... and yet nothing particular has happened.» The Rat: «Or something very surprising and splendid and beautiful».
fact: Drowsy in the boat, the Mole says the wind in the reeds is «like music—far away music».
fact: The Rat hears the reeds sing: «Lest the awe should dwell — And turn your frolic to fret — You shall look on my power at the helping hour — But then you shall forget!» (frolic = play; fret = worry).
fact: The song has three verses, each ending in forgetting; in the third the singer says «Helper and healer, I cheer — Small waifs in the woodland wet» (waifs = lost little ones), «I spring the trap that is set», and «wounds I bind».
fact: The chapter ends with the Rat «fast asleep», «with a smile of much happiness on his face»: the Mole never hears the last, «real» part of the song.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «lush» (Wales) = lovely; «sus» = suspicious; «grand» (Ireland) = fine; «sound» (Liverpool) = good, kind; «bussin'» = really good.
exercise: What dazzles the two animals on the island? | The first rays of the rising sun | The flash of a lantern from the mill-house window | The silver gleam of the weir under the moon | The lamps of a motor-car passing on the road
exercise: When they can look again, what has happened? | The Vision has vanished | The Piper is still there, smiling down at them | Portly has woken and swum off down the river | The island has sunk beneath the foaming water
exercise: What sound fills the air now? | The birds' carol, greeting the dawn | Utter silence, just as there was before | Church bells ringing in the far-off village | The poop-poop of a car on the high road
exercise: What brings the gift of forgetting? | A little breeze in their faces | A drink from a cup that the Piper holds out | A cold splash of water from the weir | A lullaby sung softly by the willows
exercise: What does the book call this gift? | The gift of forgetfulness | The gift of song | The gift of a swift river-boat | The gift of gold
exercise: Why does the kindly demi-god give this gift? | So the great memory won't spoil their happy little lives | So they can never find the island again and disturb him there | So they will forget to tell Otter where Portly had been | So they will fall straight asleep and miss their breakfast
exercise: What does «oblivion» mean? | Complete forgetting | A long deep sleep | A blinding light | A kind of small boat
exercise: What does the Rat say just after the breeze? | That this was the right sort of place to find Portly | That he has just seen a great god with his own eyes and will never forget it | That they must hurry to catch the morning train | That he is far too hungry to go one step further
exercise: How is the Mole like someone waking from a beautiful dream? | He keeps a dim sense of the beauty, then that fades too | He remembers every single detail and tells them all to the Rat at once | He is cross and wants to go straight back to sleep | He thinks the dream was dreadful and is glad it's over
exercise: How does Portly first wake? | With a joyous squeak | With a scream of fright | Without making a sound | With a big yawn and a sneeze
exercise: What does Portly do soon after he wakes? | Hunts round the island whining, then cries bitterly | Jumps into the boat and asks to be taken home at once | Dives into the weir to catch his own fish for breakfast | Curls up and falls straight back to sleep in the grass
exercise: Who is Portly searching for, as the book hints? | The one who watched over him while he slept | His mother, who had come to the island with him | The Mole, who has wandered off among the trees | The fishing-rod his father gave him at the ford
exercise: What does the Rat look at «long and doubtfully»? | Hoof-marks deep in the grass | A torn net hanging in a willow | A trap sprung open on the bank | A broken pipe lying on the lawn
exercise: «Some — great — animal — has been here.» What does this show? | The Rat half-feels something, though he has forgotten | The Rat is afraid of a bull from the farm | The Rat thinks Toad has been hiding there | The Rat knows exactly who it was and says so
exercise: How is Portly comforted? | By the promise of a jaunt in Mr. Rat's real boat | By a big plate of hot buttered toast and a mug of sweet tea | By a funny song that the Mole makes up for him | By the gift of a brand-new fishing-rod
exercise: What does «jaunt» mean? | A short trip for fun | A long, sad journey | A kind of small boat | A hot breakfast
exercise: How do the flowers look on the way back? | Less bright than something they half-remember seeing | Brighter than they had ever been all summer | Brown and dead after a sharp night frost | Exactly the same as the flowers on the island
exercise: Where do they set Portly down? | On the tow-path near the ford | On the island by the weir | At the front door of Toad Hall | On the steps of the Rat's house
exercise: What does Otter do when he sees Portly coming? | Bounds up with an amazed and joyous bark | Scolds him crossly for running away again | Pretends not to see him and goes on fishing | Faints clean away on the bank from the shock
exercise: What do the Mole and the Rat do once Otter has seen Portly? | Swing the boat round and let the stream carry them home | Land on the bank and join the otters' big breakfast | Row on upstream to look at the weir once more | Hurry off across the fields and through the Wood to tell Badger the good news
exercise: How does the Mole say he feels on the way home? | As if through something exciting and rather terrible, yet nothing has happened | Simply sleepy from the late night and nothing more | Cross and hungry and cold, and longing only for his own bed at Mole End, nothing more | Proud that he alone found Portly
exercise: «And turn your frolic to fret.» What does «frolic» mean? | Play and fun | A small frog | A kind of song | A fishing net
exercise: Which work does the singer in the reeds say he does? | Springs traps and binds the wounds of small lost creatures | Builds bridges over rivers for travelling animals | Rings the village church bells on Christmas night for all the creatures | Drives the late steam-train through the dark woods
exercise: How does the chapter end? | The Rat falls asleep smiling before he tells the last part | The Rat sings the whole song through twice to the Mole, word for word | The friends row straight to Toad Hall for a party | The Piper appears again by the old ford
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I the Rat said animal-etiquette forbids comment when friends suddenly vanish. Who vanished that day? | The Otter | The Badger | Toad | The Mole
exercise: Looking back: at the end of chapter VI, what did Badger fear might bring Toad back? | A stretcher, or two policemen | A brand-new motor-car and a chauffeur | A slow train and a kind porter | A long letter from the lawyer
test: Gwen from Cardiff: «Mole and Ratty remember every bit of the Piper for ever, it's lush.» (lush = lovely) Put it right. | A breeze brought forgetting; only a dim sense of beauty stays | Yes, and they write it all down that same morning | They remember it, but they promise never to tell | They never saw anything; Portly told them about it
test: «Kinda sus that Ratty says "some great animal". He knows!» (sus = suspicious) What is true? | He has forgotten, but the hoof-marks stir his mind | He knows and is hiding it from the Mole on purpose | He thinks a cow wandered across the weir in the night | He is making a joke about how fat Toad has got
test: Why might forgetting be called a «gift» here? | So the great awe won't overshadow their fun and happiness | Because forgetting things is always good for everyone | Because the Piper wants them to stay away from the island | Because they had far too much to remember about Toad
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | Portly cries when he cannot find the one who watched over him | Portly swims back to the ford all on his own, across the weir | Otter is angry when Portly comes home | The Rat remembers the whole song perfectly
test: Siobhan from Cork: «grand, so the Rat carries Portly right up to Otter's front door.» (grand = fine) Put it right. | They set him on the tow-path and watch him waddle to Otter | They hand him over to Badger to take home through the Wood | They leave him on the island for Otter to fetch later | They take him to Toad Hall and send a note to Otter
test: «It's like music — far away music.» Who says this, and about what? | The Mole, about the wind in the reeds | The Rat, about Toad singing in his bath | Otter, about the roar of the weir | Portly, about the birds at dawn
test: What might have happened if the animals had kept the whole memory? | The huge memory might overshadow all their play and happiness | They would have turned into gods themselves | They would have been able to fly home over the fields like the birds | Nothing would have been any different at all
test: Kev from Liverpool: «Ratty's sound, he tells Mole the whole last bit of the song.» (sound = good, kind) Put it right. | He falls asleep before he can give the last part | He tells it, but the Mole has already fallen asleep | He sings it to Otter instead, at the ford | He writes it down on paper for the Mole to read
test: «Strays I find in it, wounds I bind in it.» How does this fit Portly? | He was a lost little stray, and the Helper kept him safe | He had broken his leg and the Rat bandaged it | He had been riding in Toad's motor-car | He had a bad cold from the river water
test: «Bussin' ending: Mole hears every word of the song.» (bussin' = really good) Put it right. | Only the Rat hears words, and he falls asleep before the last part | The Mole hears every word and sings it back | Neither of them hears anything but the wind | Otter shouts the words across the water
test: Looking back: in chapter II, who sat in the road murmuring «Poop-poop!» in a happy trance? | Toad, after the motor-car wrecked the caravan | The Rat, after he heard the piping in the reeds | The old grey horse, after the cart went into the ditch | The Mole, after he smelt his old home on the road
test: Looking back: in chapter V the Rat helped the Mole quietly, without fuss. Which act in this part shows the same quiet kindness? | They let Portly go to Otter alone, and slip away | They shout the news along the whole river bank | They ask Otter for a big reward for finding him | They march Portly up to Badger to be scolded
test: Looking back: the Mole cried on a tree-stump in chapter V. Who cries in this part? | Little Portly, when he cannot find his Helper | The Mole, when the boat almost tips over | Otter, out of anger at his naughty son | The Rat, because he is hungry and cold
test: Looking back: the book is called «The Wind in the Willows». Where in this part does the wind in the reeds turn into words? | On the drift home, when the Rat hears a song in it | At the start, while the Mole lies on the dock-leaves | On the island, before the sun comes up | At the ford, when Otter starts up and barks
test: Looking back: which friend is far away, shut in a castle dungeon, all through chapter VII? | Toad | Badger | Otter | The Mole
test: ★★★ The reeds' song has three verses. How many of them end with the word «forget»? | All three | Only the first | Two of them | None of them
test: ★★★ Careful: «the kindly demi-god». What is a demi-god? | A half-god, between gods and ordinary creatures | A god who is cross and unkind to animals | A god who rules only one half of the river | A man who dresses up and pretends to be a god
test: ★★★ The Mole says «nothing particular has happened»; the Rat says «something very surprising and splendid and beautiful». Who is nearer the truth, and why can't they be sure? | The Rat; they have been made to forget what they saw | The Mole; nothing at all happened that night | Neither; they slept on the island all night long | Both; Portly told them the whole story on the way
exam: Identify the gift which, according to the narrative, the demi-god bestows upon those to whom he has revealed himself. | The gift of forgetfulness | The gift of perpetual youth and vigour | The gift of comprehending the speech of all creatures | The gift of a safe passage across the weir
exam: State the observation made by the Water Rat upon examining the ground of the island. | That some great animal had been there, as hoof-marks in the sward attested | That the grass had been trampled flat by the young otter in his play | That a trap had lately been set and sprung beside the willows | That the weasels had passed that way in considerable numbers
exam: Select the response which accurately characterises the manner of the young otter's restoration to his parent. | He was set upon the tow-path near the ford, whence he proceeded alone | He was conveyed by boat directly into his father's arms at the weir | He was escorted to the Otters' residence by the Badger in person | He swam unaided from the island to the old ford
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the chapter? | The Water Rat fell asleep before communicating the final portion of the song | The Mole recited the entire song to the Water Rat without omission | The two animals resolved to revisit the island upon the following night | The elder Otter accompanied the two animals homeward in the boat
